# Docker build context exclusions.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both Dockerfiles run `COPY . .` in their build stage. Without this file that
# copies the entire working tree — including .env — into an image layer, and
# layers are permanent: a later stage that does not carry the file forward
# does not remove it from the image's history. Anyone who can pull the image
# can `docker save` it and read RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET in plaintext.
#
# The webhook secret is the worst of the three, because this architecture
# treats a correctly signed webhook as the single source of payment truth —
# holding it means minting entitlements without paying.
#
# Keep the negation (!.env.example) after the .env* rule: order matters, and
# the example file carries no values, only key names the build may reference.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- secrets: the reason this file exists ---
.env*
!.env.example

# --- version control history (carries deleted secrets even if HEAD is clean) ---
.git
.gitignore

# --- dependencies: reinstalled inside the image from the committed lockfile ---
# Also a large speed win: these never need to enter the build context.
node_modules
.pnpm-store

# --- build output and caches: rebuilt inside the image ---
.turbo
.next
dist
build

# --- test and coverage artefacts ---
coverage

# --- logs, which routinely contain request payloads and identifiers ---
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# --- local tooling that no build step reads ---
.DS_Store
.vscode
.idea

# --- local database files ---
packages/db/prisma/dev.db

# DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED, because the build needs them:
#   package.json, pnpm-lock.yaml, pnpm-workspace.yaml, turbo.json
#   tsconfig*.json, apps/**, packages/**, scripts/**
#   packages/db/prisma/schema.prisma and its migrations
